  "query_preview": "..\n\nKEY MESSAGES:\n\n- Rivers, streams, and creeks continue to run high across \n  northwest Montana from prolonged rain and ongoing snowmelt.\n\n- Drying trend later today into Wednesday morning. \n\n- Showers and thunderstorms return Wednesday evening. \n\nRadar and surface observations this morning show widespread rain\nacross northwest Montana, primarily along and east of US-93. This\nrain continues to be driven by a closed low spinning east of the\ndivide. This is the same low responsible for significant\nprecipitation totals the last 4 days. Area rain gauges show 4-6\ninches of precipitation across the Mission and Swan Ranges, with\n~1.50-3.00 inches falling across the Flathead and Mission Valleys.\nLocally higher totals have been reported on the east side of  \nFlathead Lake, with over 4.3 inches of rain observed in the Bear\nHollow area. As rain continues to fall, an Areal Flood Warning \nremains in effect for the Flathead and Mission valleys through \nthis morning for minor flooding and ponding of water from small \nstreams and creeks along the western slopes of the Mission and \nSwan mountain ranges. An Areal Flood Watch remains for the Glacier\nPark region for small stream flooding and a heightened risk for \nrock/mud slides.\n\nModel guidance has the low finally pushing eastward this\nafternoon, with shower activity gradually diminishing across\nnorthwest Montana by mid-afternoon into the evening. High \npressure will briefly build across the region through Wednesday \nmorning. Clearing skies tonight into Wednesday morning, will set \nthe stage for widespread fog development, focused along and north \nof the I-90 corridor. \n\nThe next system arrives Wednesday evening, as a Pacific trough\nmoves across the Pacific Northwest into the Northern Rockies. This\nsystem will not bring high precipitation totals as with the last\nsystem. Model guidance has been trending towards an open wave\ntrough, with numerous showers and isolated thunderstorms. The \nthreat for severe, organized convection is less than 5 percent, as\nwind shear and instability profiles do not support strong to \nsevere thunderstorms. Nonetheless, storms will be capable of \nlightning, small hail, and brief moderate rainfall. The trough \nwill push along and east of the divide by Thursday. Shower and \nstorm activity will focus along the divide on Thursday. \n\nEnsemble guidance shows another dry period on Friday under high \npressure. By the weekend, ensemble guidance has a stronger low \npressure system moving into the Pacific Northwest. Model solutions \ndiverge on how this low pressure system will move across the \nNorthern Rockies Saturday into Monday. As discussed previously, \nguidance has been trending towards the low stretching and \nsplitting, with the bulk of the dynamics and precipitation moving\ninto Canada and a weak feature moving southward into California. \nEnsemble cluster analysis suggests this solution is favored by 75%\nof guidance, with the remaining 25% showing a consolidated low \nthrough the Northern Rockies with widespread precipitation. The \ngeneral consensus (75% of members) is for scattered showers, with\nlight precipitation totals.",
